  multiple forms" names structure this repo doesn't have yet: a validation module and consumers that don't exist.
  There's no existing multi-form flow to modify, so I'll run the full process (questions → approaches → design → spec)
  rather than present a short in-chat design. Say the word if you'd rather I keep it lightweight.

  First question, and it's the one that shapes everything else.

  The fork: an abstraction extracted for forms you can actually name comes out fitted; one extracted for hypothetical
  forms comes out either over-general or wrong. Right now the only rule in the codebase is "field is non-empty," which
  is too thin to generalize from on its own.

  - [1mSpecific forms in mind[0m — you name them (signup, profile, checkout…) and I design against their real rules. Best fit,
    no guessing. Costs you a minute of listing them.
  - [1mRich rules, forms TBD[0m — you know the [3mkinds[0m of rules coming (email format, min length, password confirmation, numeric
    ranges) even if not the screens. Design targets rule variety instead of specific forms; slightly more general than
    strictly needed today.
  - [1mJust de-duplicate what exists[0m — pull [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m out of [38;5;153mapp.js[39m into a shared module with a required-fields check
    and stop. Smallest change, genuinely useful, and cheap to extend later — but it won't answer "how do I express an
    email rule" until someone needs one.

  My recommendation: the first, if you can name even two forms. Validation abstractions are notoriously easy to
  over-build, and real rules from real forms are the only reliable guard against that. If you can't name them yet, the
  third is a better default than the second — extracting a thin module now costs almost nothing and doesn't lock in a
  rule-expression format you'd have to undo.
